3c0i,\u03c0j\u223c\u03a0\u2032 [yri,j \u00b7 log(y\u0302ri,j) + (1\u2212 yri,j) \u00b7 log(1\u2212 y\u0302ri,j).] (7)\nThe complete training procedures of SOCCER is summarized in Algorithm 1. In practice, to reduce the computational cost brought by CL, we set \u03b6r as a linear projection followed by a sigmoid function. Therefore, the number of additional parameters only grows linearly with the number of annotators. Note that the CL is only available during training since we still use y\u0302i,j to generate the predicted ranking of test policies.\nAlgorithm 1: SOCCER Training Data: Offline Trajectory set D, Policy set \u03a0, OPE worker setW . Result: Policy comparison transformer g . N \u2190 n; # The number of trainning epochs K \u2190 k; # The number of sampled states Initialize the transformer g; Initialize the aggregation token e0; Initialize the policy positional embeddings e\u03b1 and e\u03b2 ; Initialize the token encoder f ; Initialize the linear projection function \u03d5; Initialize the crowd layer \u03be; # Initialize parameters while N \u0338= 0 do\nSample 2 policies: \u03c0i, \u03c0j \u223c \u03a0; Sample K states: s1, s2, \u00b7 \u00b7 \u00b7 , sK \u223c D; Take actions by both policies: aik \u223c \u03c0i(\u00b7|sk), anj \u223c \u03c0j(\u00b7|sk); Construct a pairwise policy decision sequence: \u03c4i,j =< (s1, a i 1), (s1, a j 1), \u00b7 \u00b7 \u00b7 , (sK , aiK), (sK , a j K) >; Encode state-action pairs into input tokens: xik = f(sk, a i k),x j k = f(sk, a j k); Add policy positional embeddings to tokens: z0 = [e0,xi1 + e\u03b1,x j 1 + e\u03b2 , \u00b7 \u00b7 \u00b7xiK + e\u03b1,x j K + e\u03b2 ] Get the pairwise policy representation: \u03c7i,j = f(z0); Get the probability of whether \u03c0i performs better than \u03c0j : y\u0302i,j = o(\u03c7i,j); for each OPE worker r \u2208 W do\nGenerate the noisy labels: yri,j = 1Rri>Rrj ; Get the estimated noisy labels by crowd layer \u03ber: y\u0302ri,j = \u03be\nr(y\u0302i,j); Compute the loss according